WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.880
Guys, we're locking in.

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.720
We're dopamine maxing today. If you find

00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:08.040
it hard to focus or to be productive,

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:10.000
literally all typical one-size-fits-all

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:12.600
advice is not built for you because that

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:14.360
advice is for people without attention

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:16.120
deficit and you are built different.

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:18.360
Especially if you're neurodivergent, you

00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:20.640
have ADHD, but to say that we can't

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:22.760
focus ever, that's not true at all. Our

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:25.200
attention is incredible. It's almost

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:28.120
genius-level insane, actually. But the

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:29.720
reason why ADHD people fail is because

00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:31.800
we're holding ourselves to neurotypical

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:33.440
standards, which is not for us. I have

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:35.080
so many tips for you in this video

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:37.000
that's going to change your life or at

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:38.800
the very least it's going to inspire

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:41.920
you. So, I was finally diagnosed with

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:44.080
ADHD only last year and I suspected this

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:46.160
for a while, but for most of my life I

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:48.400
was struggling and constantly thinking

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:50.320
something's wrong with me, but somehow I

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:52.000
always somehow made it through with

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:54.120
multiple university degrees, I've gotten

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:56.160
multiple promotions at my jobs. So, I

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:59.320
know how to survive this, but because I

00:00:56.160 --> 00:01:01.560
didn't know it was ADHD, I would also

00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:03.960
get in my own the time. So, then I have

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:05.400
to work twice as hard as everybody else.

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:06.760
I know I'm smart because I can solve

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:08.560
problems really fast. I'm good with

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:10.480
pattern recognition. I became a Mensa

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:12.520
member when I was 14. And in fact,

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:14.680
almost everyone I know with ADHD are

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:16.280
also super smart, but for some reason

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:17.600
they have something blocking them.

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:19.880
Because it's hard for us to stay

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:21.520
consistent and that's actually a sign

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:23.320
that the system that you're using

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:25.160
doesn't match your life. But because our

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:26.800
neurodivergent brains look very

00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:29.480
different from a neurotypical brain when

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:32.040
it comes to one, executive function, and

00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:33.480
two, time blindness. Even just saying,

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:35.720
"Go make a to-do list." or "Go take a

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:36.840
walk." may not work so well for us. So,

00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:38.120
if you want to be able to function

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:39.520
better, have better time management

00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:41.800
skills, we're going to have to know

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:44.200
ourselves and build our own system. So,

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:46.960
ADHD brains typically rely more heavily

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:49.040
on stimulus-driven attention rather than

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:51.160
goal-driven attention. That means our

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:52.840
brains work the best when we are

00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:53.920
stimulated and there are all kinds of

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:56.000
ways we can use this to become

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:57.560
superhuman. I know for a fact that if we

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:59.520
are super passionate about something,

00:01:57.560 --> 00:02:01.440
literally nothing can stop us. My name

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:03.160
is May. My community is called Stoic

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:05.800
Angels, and we're all about becoming the

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:07.160
best versions of ourselves, so to speak.

00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:09.000
My group trip to the Greek Islands is

00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:11.320
coming up very soon, actually, this

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:12.640
July, and I only have a few more spots.

00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:14.120
I've been seeing a lot of wheels

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:15.560
turning, people thinking about it. So,

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:16.920
if you're considering coming along, like

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:19.200
literally, there's three more rooms

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:21.400
left, like maybe two now, because it's

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:23.120
three as of me filming this today. I

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:25.120
have chapters in this video, as well as

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:27.000
a PDF guide on how to become your most

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:29.400
productive self. It's a companion guide

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:31.120
to this video. So, if you struggle with

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:32.640
your attention span, it's free for you

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:34.080
to download, and you can download that

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:35.360
in the link in the description. Before

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:36.680
we get started, and this is super

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:38.440
relevant, actually, I want to thank

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:40.040
today's sponsor, Skillshare. Skillshare

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:41.920
is an online learning platform with

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:43.640
thousands of classes on all kinds of

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:45.640
topics, creativity, business,

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:47.240
productivity, and personal development.

00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:49.480
What I like about Skillshare is that the

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:51.040
classes are structured in small

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:52.760
segments. You can take a class with 10

