Notion, Canva, Pinterest, and ChatGPT.

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:47.080
And the reason why I love this for us is

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:48.840
that the instructor is not handing you

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:50.800
one single way of doing things, and then

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:52.480
be like, "Do this." She's teaching you

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:55.040
how to build a system that fits your own

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:56.480
unique brain based on your goals. So, if

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:59.000
you want to check out Skillshare, I will

00:03:56.480 --> 00:04:00.520
give you a month free to try it out

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:02.200
where you can access everything. The

00:04:00.520 --> 00:04:03.560
first 500 people to use my link in the

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:05.000
description will get a free trial of

00:04:03.560 --> 00:04:06.880
Skillshare. Let's talk about the two

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:08.440
things we struggle the most, energy and

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:10.320
time. For neurotypicals, they can

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:11.800
sustain energy much better than we can.

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:13.880
Their energy levels are simply more

00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:16.000
stable, so with our executive

00:04:13.880 --> 00:04:17.600
dysfunction, there are days where I

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:19.040
don't even know I'm going to get. I may

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:21.600
have the zoomies all day long and I

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:23.560
still cannot set a task because our

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:26.000
ability to regulate dopamine is

00:04:23.560 --> 00:04:28.120
different. Dopamine is heavily involved

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:30.160
in motivation, attention, and reward

00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:31.560
processing. So, if we're not interested

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:33.120
in something, we're not even going to do

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:35.600
it. And if we don't do it, we start to

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:37.520
feel guilty. So, whatever we do needs to

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:39.120
feel interesting to us. The human brain

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:41.760
is actually very uncomfortable with

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:44.000
leaving things undone. Your job is not

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:45.400
to write the report because that sounds

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:47.240
like, I don't know, maybe not

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:48.880
interesting to you, but to literally, if

00:04:47.240 --> 00:04:51.440
you want to start this project, just go

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:53.320
and sit at your desk. That's it. One

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:55.919
thing will always lead to another, and

00:04:53.320 --> 00:04:59.080
even if it just looks like this,

00:04:55.919 --> 00:05:00.400
I'm doing it. I'm doing it. It's fine.

00:04:59.080 --> 00:05:02.960
Your brain is now going to want to do

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:04.440
the rest. Dopamine delay is also great.

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:06.960
Like, if I mopped the floor today, I

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:08.760
will get a little treat. Even if I only

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:11.480
want the little treat and I don't know

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:13.200
what to do, I will find something to do.

00:05:11.480 --> 00:05:15.080
Then, I will get the treat, and that's

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:16.880
how I train myself to delay dopamine. If

00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:18.600
it's boring, then we need a carrot on

00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:20.840
the stick literally in front of us all

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:22.640
the time. And gamify things. I did 10

00:05:20.840 --> 00:05:25.280
things a day, so that's 10 points. For

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:26.880
10 points, I can now go open the package

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:28.240
that came in the mail today. Yay. So,

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:29.919
for whatever your version of that will

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:32.080
look like, you can write that down, too,

00:05:29.919 --> 00:05:34.200
and that can be your dopamine menu. For

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:36.840
extremely stimulating things, however, I

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:39.160
try to be very careful to not use it as

00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:41.480
a reward. So, know what yours look like,

00:05:39.160 --> 00:05:43.600
the danger zone. For me, that's going to

00:05:41.480 --> 00:05:45.720
be video games. That's a big one. I will

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:47.640
only play video games once I'm

00:05:45.720 --> 00:05:49.400
absolutely done with everything that I

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:51.560
want to do for the day, and even then

00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:53.200
it's rare. I try not to because I'm such

00:05:51.560 --> 00:05:55.040
a lock-in girl, I would just lock in on

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:57.280
video games for like months. Another

00:05:55.040 --> 00:05:59.280
thing about ADHD brains is we typically

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:01.360
get an energy surge at night, and it

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:04.080
peaks at around 10:00 or 11:00 p.m. So,

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:06.400
if we're up at this time doing very

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:08.880
stimulating things, before you know it,

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:11.280
it's going to be 3:00 a.m. In the blink

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:13.880
of an eye, our ability to perceive time

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:15.720
is also very different. People have 24

00:06:13.880 --> 00:06:17.840
hours in a day that's neurotypical, but

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:19.560
for me it's more like 6 hours because I

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:21.520
process time really quickly, which is

00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:23.200
also why I talk so fast. And guys, I'm

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:25.520
working on it. One way for us to get

00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:27.560
past this time blindness is to know

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:30.000
that, first of all, digital clocks are

00:06:27.560 --> 00:06:31.960
not good for us. We don't really compute

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:33.800
it that well, and I have to say compute

00:06:31.960 --> 00:06:36.000
because we literally need to program our

00:06:33.800 --> 00:06:38.280
brains with triggers. For example, a

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:40.320
visual analog clock is so much better

00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:42.440
for us. Neurodivergent people are more

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:44.520
visual, we're more sensorial, we really

00:06:42.440 --> 00:06:46.720
need to get the full picture, otherwise

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:48.640
it's confusing. Another way is to think

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:50.760
about time differently. We respond to

00:06:48.640 --> 00:06:53.000
much shorter deadlines. It needs to feel

00:06:50.760 --> 00:06:54.919
urgent, but in a smart way that isn't

00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:57.440
stressful for us. So, if your fitness

00:06:54.919 --> 00:07:00.080
class is at 9:00 a.m., the real time for

00:06:57.440 --> 00:07:02.280
you is the time that you're leaving the

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:03.720
house and you're already prepared. So,

00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:06.080
if it's 20 minutes away, your fitness

00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:07.240
class is at 8:40 a.m., no exceptions.

00:07:06.080 --> 00:07:09.200
You need to know how much time it takes

00:07:07.240 --> 00:07:11.400
to get ready, to pack, if there's

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:13.040
traffic, but because we can also lose

00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:14.360
track of time. So, time yourself when

00:07:13.040 --> 00:07:16.600
you do all of these things. Like, for

00:07:14.360 --> 00:07:18.600
example, I assume it takes 5 minutes to

00:07:16.600 --> 00:07:20.720
get ready, right? But really it's 20

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:22.080
minutes. 20 minutes to get there, 20

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:25.000
minutes to get ready, not 5 minutes. So,

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:26.960
now your fitness class is at 8:20. Not

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:29.520
masking helps as well so you're not

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:31.840
draining your energy even more. I tell

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:33.840
people I have ADHD and I have my quirks

00:07:31.840 --> 00:07:35.400
so that they can anticipate it. If they

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:37.320
discriminate against you for some

00:07:35.400 --> 00:07:39.560
reason, in a lot of places that's like

00:07:37.320 --> 00:07:41.840
illegal. So, play it by ear, right? If

00:07:39.560 --> 00:07:43.440
people around you are good people, then

00:07:41.840 --> 00:07:45.680
they will understand. So, this person I

00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:47.480
work with a lot, we both have ADHD, so

00:07:45.680 --> 00:07:48.840
we're always just being neurodivergent

00:07:47.480 --> 00:07:50.720
with each other, and it helps to have

00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:51.880
people understand you. Like, for

00:07:50.720 --> 00:07:53.600
example, you can tell your boss, "Hey

00:07:51.880 --> 00:07:55.480
boss, I work really well after dinner

00:07:53.600 --> 00:07:57.320
for some reason, so I'm going to take

00:07:55.480 --> 00:07:59.600
this project home." But, try and take as

00:07:57.320 --> 00:08:01.080
much flexibility as you can get, and I

00:07:59.600 --> 00:08:03.000
don't know, with work culture, I don't

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:04.200
think they'll say no to you working when

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:05.560
you're off the clock. Even though that's

00:08:04.200 --> 00:08:07.280
not healthy, do what works for you, but

00:08:05.560 --> 00:08:08.880
just know that this flexibility really

00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:10.800
helps you because we're capable of

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:12.000
delivering typical way of doing things

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:14.480
doesn't really work that well for you.

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:16.000
For example, a 9-to-5 doesn't work well

00:08:14.480 --> 00:08:18.760
for most people, especially the

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:20.680
neurodivergent. So, buy as much time and

00:08:18.760 --> 00:08:22.600
energy as you can. That means eating

00:08:20.680 --> 00:08:23.960
healthy, sleeping well, having a good

00:08:22.600 --> 00:08:26.040
routine so we can run things on

00:08:23.960 --> 00:08:27.840
autopilot. I made a whole video about

00:08:26.040 --> 00:08:29.440
energy reset recently, which will help

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:31.040
you with that. You also need to know

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:32.560
when to stop working so hard. Know your

00:08:31.040 --> 00:08:34.520
strengths, and then play to your

00:08:32.560 --> 00:08:36.400
strengths as much as you can. And I say

00:08:34.520 --> 00:08:38.520
working so hard because I know a lot of

00:08:36.400 --> 00:08:41.280
us work really hard. So, it will also

00:08:38.520 --> 00:08:42.520
help you to be the most likable person

00:08:41.280 --> 00:08:44.320
in your environment, like in your

00:08:42.520 --> 00:08:46.640
office, for example, especially by your

00:08:44.320 --> 00:08:48.240
boss. This is really important because

00:08:46.640 --> 00:08:50.000
long before I was diagnosed, I had no

00:08:48.240 --> 00:08:52.280
idea. I thought I was normal, but then

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:54.120
it didn't make sense to me because I can

00:08:52.280 --> 00:08:56.440
never finish anything despite working so

00:08:54.120 --> 00:08:58.600
hard. And because I'm busy working hard,

00:08:56.440 --> 00:09:00.240
I wasn't socializing enough. My

00:08:58.600 --> 00:09:01.760
coworkers thought I was ignoring them,

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:03.280
so they didn't like me. That affects

00:09:01.760 --> 00:09:04.839
your job performance, too, by the way,

00:09:03.280 --> 00:09:06.120
because you have to work with them. And

00:09:04.839 --> 00:09:07.880
you don't even have to do this very

00:09:06.120 --> 00:09:09.960
much, but whatever interactions you have

00:09:07.880 --> 00:09:12.480
with people who you rely on to be

00:09:09.960 --> 00:09:13.680
productive, make it high quality.

00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:15.280
Whatever that looks like for you, maybe

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:16.520
you can smile at them, say hello to

00:09:15.280 --> 00:09:18.400
them, try to get comfortable with

00:09:16.520 --> 00:09:19.880
telling them your weird little silly

00:09:18.400 --> 00:09:21.839
meme jokes to the people you work with.

00:09:19.880 --> 00:09:24.680
Like, they probably need to laugh, too.

00:09:21.839 --> 00:09:26.480
We all need to laugh. In fact, be the

00:09:24.680 --> 00:09:29.280
jester of the office. Jester's

00:09:26.480 --> 00:09:31.280
privilege, seriously, can go a long way.

00:09:29.280 --> 00:09:33.280
I've seen it over and over again in the

00:09:31.280 --> 00:09:35.640
offices I worked in. People who tend to

00:09:33.280 --> 00:09:37.560
be very silly and weird, they have that

00:09:35.640 --> 00:09:39.640
kind of privilege of being like a highly

00:09:37.560 --> 00:09:41.200
likable person. This will also work as

00:09:39.640 --> 00:09:43.120
well if you're going to school or

00:09:41.200 --> 00:09:44.840
whatever environment you're in that

00:09:43.120 --> 00:09:46.720
needs you to be productive. I also like

00:09:44.840 --> 00:09:48.720
to think of managing ADHD energy as

00:09:46.720 --> 00:09:50.200
riding the wave. What does that mean?

00:09:48.720 --> 00:09:51.960
You need to know what your ups and downs

00:09:50.200 --> 00:09:54.440
look like. Your brain runs on dopamine

00:09:51.960 --> 00:09:56.920
cycles and not a clock, so you need to

00:09:54.440 --> 00:09:59.080
ride that cycle. If you're up and your

00:09:56.920 --> 00:10:01.320
brain is activated, you ride that wave

00:09:59.080 --> 00:10:03.480
and you do all the hard stuff, the stuff

00:10:01.320 --> 00:10:05.040
that takes a lot of processing power.

00:10:03.480 --> 00:10:06.640
Personally, I like to work on a weekend

00:10:05.040 --> 00:10:08.040
while everyone else is chilling because

00:10:06.640 --> 00:10:10.240
I don't want anyone to bother me while

00:10:08.040 --> 00:10:12.840
I'm working. So, when you know you have

00:10:10.240 --> 00:10:14.840
good momentum, act on it. Lock in.

00:10:12.840 --> 00:10:16.480
Whatever weird thing you have to do, do

00:10:14.840 --> 00:10:18.040
it. Our brains are already weird, so we

00:10:16.480 --> 00:10:20.440
get to be weird, too. We get a get out

00:10:18.040 --> 00:10:22.600
of free jail card. So, the ends will

00:10:20.440 --> 00:10:24.760
justify the means. If you have a weird

00:10:22.600 --> 00:10:26.360
sleep schedule, you know what? So be it.

00:10:24.760 --> 00:10:27.840
As long as it's consistent, you don't

00:10:26.360 --> 00:10:30.240
have to go to sleep at 9:00 and wake up

00:10:27.840 --> 00:10:32.280
at 5:00 every day. We are slightly

00:10:30.240 --> 00:10:34.160
nocturnal anyway, but I make sure at

00:10:32.280 --> 00:10:36.480
least that the time that I go to bed and

00:10:34.160 --> 00:10:38.520
wake up is about the same. If you feel

00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:40.360
like your dopamine levels are down and

00:10:38.520 --> 00:10:42.200
you crash, you can also switch to what I

00:10:40.360 --> 00:10:43.680
call mindless tasks, stuff that doesn't

00:10:42.200 --> 00:10:46.000
need a ton of brain processing. So,

00:10:43.680 --> 00:10:48.480
usually, that's something that I enjoy

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:51.160
or that's easy, like scrolling or

00:10:48.480 --> 00:10:53.320
looking at design inspo. I do a lot of

00:10:51.160 --> 00:10:55.560
work on my phone, on my laptop. So, if

00:10:53.320 --> 00:10:57.240
it's fun and interesting, eventually, I

00:10:55.560 --> 00:10:59.240
will also move on to more productive

00:10:57.240 --> 00:11:01.000
things, but if I can't even focus on

00:10:59.240 --> 00:11:02.880
doing what I like, I'm going to take a

00:11:01.000 --> 00:11:04.680
break. Not for 5 minutes, though. Like,

00:11:02.880 --> 00:11:07.160
I don't know what 5 minutes means, and

00:11:04.680 --> 00:11:09.640
it sounds stressful, but I know what the

00:11:07.160 --> 00:11:12.320
foreseeable future means. Like, I know

00:11:09.640 --> 00:11:14.000
what an indefinite break looks like. So,

00:11:12.320 --> 00:11:15.360
trust me, like half an hour later, I

00:11:14.000 --> 00:11:17.080
will be back to doing something else.

00:11:15.360 --> 00:11:19.480
That's just how our brain works. If I

00:11:17.080 --> 00:11:21.480
can't even scroll and nothing's terribly

00:11:19.480 --> 00:11:23.800
urgent, then I'll just lay down and

00:11:21.480 --> 00:11:26.400
close my eyes and meditate and try and

00:11:23.800 --> 00:11:28.280
think about nothing and rest. Then, you

00:11:26.400 --> 00:11:30.320
can go back to doing whatever, but if

00:11:28.280 --> 00:11:32.360
you have trouble quieting your mind when

00:11:30.320 --> 00:11:34.560
you rest, that's fine, too. Just let

00:11:32.360 --> 00:11:36.320
your mind wander off, and you'll

00:11:34.560 --> 00:11:38.520
probably come up with something genius.

00:11:36.320 --> 00:11:40.400
That's an ADHD superpower. Guys, have

00:11:38.520 --> 00:11:42.960
you ever seen this meme? Like, change

00:11:40.400 --> 00:11:45.240
this to ADHD people. Like, better yet,

00:11:42.960 --> 00:11:46.640
girls with ADHD. That's why they try to

00:11:45.240 --> 00:11:48.520
stop us, because we're like double

00:11:46.640 --> 00:11:50.440
genius. Another thing is, ADHD people

00:11:48.520 --> 00:11:52.880
are very sensitive to resistance and

00:11:50.440 --> 00:11:55.040
friction and even rejection. Anything

00:11:52.880 --> 00:11:57.680
that makes it difficult for you will

00:11:55.040 --> 00:11:59.520
become a barrier between you and getting

00:11:57.680 --> 00:12:01.720
something done. For example, if your gym

00:11:59.520 --> 00:12:04.040
is 45 minutes away by car and it's far,

00:12:01.720 --> 00:12:05.920
you will eventually stop going. If your

00:12:04.040 --> 00:12:08.280
pots and pans are in the highest

00:12:05.920 --> 00:12:10.360
cabinet, you will probably not cook. And

00:12:08.280 --> 00:12:12.560
if you're hard on yourself thinking like

00:12:10.360 --> 00:12:14.080
you're a loser or you have imposter

00:12:12.560 --> 00:12:16.480
syndrome or you you think everybody

00:12:14.080 --> 00:12:19.360
hates you, then you're going to shy away

00:12:16.480 --> 00:12:22.200
from getting things done. So, please, be

00:12:19.360 --> 00:12:24.680
extra kind to yourself. This is also why

00:12:22.200 --> 00:12:27.200
the most effective ADHD productivity

00:12:24.680 --> 00:12:29.240
systems focus on removing steps,

00:12:27.200 --> 00:12:31.640
editing, cross things out before you

00:12:29.240 --> 00:12:33.160
even start, and make simple and gentle

00:12:31.640 --> 00:12:34.960
for you as possible. Here's one thing I

00:12:33.160 --> 00:12:37.080
like to do. If I do have a to-do list,

00:12:34.960 --> 00:12:39.440
which I usually don't, I will find like

00:12:37.080 --> 00:12:41.640
10 things to do, and I will cross out

00:12:39.440 --> 00:12:43.360
seven things so that I'm only focusing

00:12:41.640 --> 00:12:45.440
on the top three things. And if I'm done

00:12:43.360 --> 00:12:48.080
with those three things, then maybe I

00:12:45.440 --> 00:12:49.760
can consider doing two more and then two

00:12:48.080 --> 00:12:51.240
more and so on. You can also work on

00:12:49.760 --> 00:12:52.680
reframing your thinking. Thinking things

00:12:51.240 --> 00:12:55.120
like, "Oh, it's not that hard. Like, you

00:12:52.680 --> 00:12:56.880
know you're smart. You can do that." Or,

00:12:55.120 --> 00:12:58.680
"They all hate me because I'm a diva.

00:12:56.880 --> 00:13:00.880
They fear me." So, whatever you need to

00:12:58.680 --> 00:13:02.920
do to reframe, and then whatever you

00:13:00.880 --> 00:13:05.240
can't deal with, try and automate it.

00:13:02.920 --> 00:13:06.839
For example, I hate vacuuming the floor

00:13:05.240 --> 00:13:09.120
every day, and I have two cats. I'm

00:13:06.839 --> 00:13:11.080
always cleaning and mopping. So,

00:13:09.120 --> 00:13:12.880
recently, I got a robot vacuum that does

00:13:11.080 --> 00:13:14.520
that for me. And every product that I

00:13:12.880 --> 00:13:17.240
mentioned is linked in my description,

00:13:14.520 --> 00:13:19.400
by the way. So, now all I have to do is

00:13:17.240 --> 00:13:21.560
a bit of maintenance work. I also set up

00:13:19.400 --> 00:13:23.680
a bunch of things running on a schedule.

00:13:21.560 --> 00:13:25.200
I have an Alexa routine. If your inbox

00:13:23.680 --> 00:13:28.560
is overwhelming to you when you look at

00:13:25.200 --> 00:13:30.320
it every day, use tags, labels, filters,

00:13:28.560 --> 00:13:32.120
different email addresses for different

00:13:30.320 --> 00:13:34.280
things. So, you can just set it up one

00:13:32.120 --> 00:13:36.840
time and then forget about it. I

00:13:34.280 --> 00:13:39.520
absolutely also do not start work unless

00:13:36.840 --> 00:13:42.440
my house is clean. A clean space is a

00:13:39.520 --> 00:13:44.400
clean mind. If your desk is messy,

00:13:42.440 --> 00:13:46.040
that's a lot of visual friction. You

00:13:44.400 --> 00:13:47.880
will literally find it harder to be

00:13:46.040 --> 00:13:49.920
productive. If there are a bunch of

00:13:47.880 --> 00:13:52.760
projects that you want to work on, write

00:13:49.920 --> 00:13:54.400
everything down with no expectation that

00:13:52.760 --> 00:13:55.920
you will have to work on any of them.

00:13:54.400 --> 00:13:58.600
This is not going to be a to-do list for

00:13:55.920 --> 00:14:00.080
you. This is a stuff you can do list.

00:13:58.600 --> 00:14:01.440
Chances are you have all kinds of great

00:14:00.080 --> 00:14:02.840
ideas. So, it's better to just have them

00:14:01.440 --> 00:14:04.840
all in front of you. Otherwise, you will

00:14:02.840 --> 00:14:07.240
forget. Another weird quirk that I have

00:14:04.840 --> 00:14:10.120
as someone with ADHD is I hate anything

00:14:07.240 --> 00:14:12.480
with lines on them or any kind of like

00:14:10.120 --> 00:14:14.240
templates that's extremely ready-made or

00:14:12.480 --> 00:14:16.440
whatever it is. If it's simple or

00:14:14.240 --> 00:14:17.840
something that I make, it may work. But,

00:14:16.440 --> 00:14:19.880
you may have to also create something

00:14:17.840 --> 00:14:22.000
for yourself. So, which is why on my

00:14:19.880 --> 00:14:23.880
merch shop, I sell my practice notebook.

00:14:22.000 --> 00:14:26.000
And it's actually a dotted grid

00:14:23.880 --> 00:14:27.440
notebook. Every day, I'm writing like

00:14:26.000 --> 00:14:29.880
all kinds of stuff. I'm writing

00:14:27.440 --> 00:14:31.680
diagonally. I'm drawing different lines.

00:14:29.880 --> 00:14:32.880
I'm creating my own systems. And then

00:14:31.680 --> 00:14:35.800
when you can't be bothered with creating

00:14:32.880 --> 00:14:37.760
any systems, you just do a good old word

00:14:35.800 --> 00:14:40.040
dump. Just dumping whatever comes into

00:14:37.760 --> 00:14:42.360
your head onto a notebook. We also

00:14:40.040 --> 00:14:44.360
struggle with this one thing. I try to

00:14:42.360 --> 00:14:45.920
schedule everything in the day as early

00:14:44.360 --> 00:14:48.280
as possible. But, if you have one

00:14:45.920 --> 00:14:49.520
meeting at 4:00 p.m., then the rest of

00:14:48.280 --> 00:14:51.520
your day might feel like you're just

00:14:49.520 --> 00:14:53.839
waiting for that meeting. Instead,

00:14:51.520 --> 00:14:56.160
prepare for the meeting first and get

00:14:53.839 --> 00:14:58.240
the preparation out of the way before

00:14:56.160 --> 00:15:00.360
they happen. For me, I will create an

00:14:58.240 --> 00:15:01.760
agenda of things I want to talk about. I

00:15:00.360 --> 00:15:03.760
will look up the company that I'm

00:15:01.760 --> 00:15:05.960
speaking with, for example, or whoever

00:15:03.760 --> 00:15:07.880
I'm speaking to, which will help erase

00:15:05.960 --> 00:15:09.400
all of my anxiety so that I'm just not

00:15:07.880 --> 00:15:12.280
waiting around for the meeting to

00:15:09.400 --> 00:15:14.800
happen. Not being prepared will feel

00:15:12.280 --> 00:15:17.040
messy and it will make us anxious, which

00:15:14.800 --> 00:15:18.560
is why we're just sitting around feeling

00:15:17.040 --> 00:15:20.000
restless waiting for this to happen. And

00:15:18.560 --> 00:15:22.200
I promise you you will not have so much

00:15:20.000 --> 00:15:25.120
anxiety if you are at least self-assured

00:15:22.200 --> 00:15:27.520
of your own outcome. So, keep isolating

00:15:25.120 --> 00:15:29.320
and reducing and edit as much as you can

00:15:27.520 --> 00:15:30.800
so that nothing stands between you and

00:15:29.320 --> 00:15:33.280
what you need to do. Even if it looks

00:15:30.800 --> 00:15:35.680
like a messy word dump at first, that's

00:15:33.280 --> 00:15:37.640
fine. We can always edit it. Then, once

00:15:35.680 --> 00:15:39.680
you make it super simple to start doing

00:15:37.640 --> 00:15:41.400
the thing will help us create more and

00:15:39.680 --> 00:15:43.520
more momentum and I promise you a lot of

00:15:41.400 --> 00:15:45.480
that anxiety that we feel constantly is

00:15:43.520 --> 00:15:48.200
because we're worrying about not doing

00:15:45.480 --> 00:15:50.200
the thing. So, the cure is to do it or

00:15:48.200 --> 00:15:51.840
at least know what's coming up. There's

00:15:50.200 --> 00:15:53.600
no other way around this. So, just be

00:15:51.840 --> 00:15:55.480
kind to yourself. And then once it's

00:15:53.600 --> 00:15:57.080
done, you can start collecting proof and

00:15:55.480 --> 00:15:59.440
evidence that you can do things like,

00:15:57.080 --> 00:16:00.760
"See, it's not that bad." That will make

00:15:59.440 --> 00:16:02.000
it much easier rather than beating

00:16:00.760 --> 00:16:04.840
yourself up. Remember, you're built

00:16:02.000 --> 00:16:06.720
different. Hyperfocus is a gift. Like,

00:16:04.840 --> 00:16:08.280
you know how we can get so locked into

00:16:06.720 --> 00:16:10.520
doom scrolling that we think that it's a

00:16:08.280 --> 00:16:12.960
curse. It's not, honey. I can create a

00:16:10.520 --> 00:16:15.680
project from start to finish in a couple

00:16:12.960 --> 00:16:17.760
of hours as long as I'm locked in. So,

00:16:15.680 --> 00:16:20.000
hyperfocus is a state of intense

00:16:17.760 --> 00:16:22.400
concentration on the task that feels

00:16:20.000 --> 00:16:24.000
constantly stimulating or rewarding. If

00:16:22.400 --> 00:16:25.440
you use this correctly, you will become

00:16:24.000 --> 00:16:27.600
the god of being productive. I'm not

00:16:25.440 --> 00:16:29.960
even joking. Ideally, that's also when

00:16:27.600 --> 00:16:31.640
you're in flow state and you become so

00:16:29.960 --> 00:16:34.160
hypnotized by your work. How do we

00:16:31.640 --> 00:16:36.320
achieve this? This is the formula. High

00:16:34.160 --> 00:16:38.760
dopamine meets strong interest. It's

00:16:36.320 --> 00:16:41.120
like a math equation. And this is why so

00:16:38.760 --> 00:16:43.120
many entrepreneurs and creators like

00:16:41.120 --> 00:16:45.200
myself and innovators, we are all

00:16:43.120 --> 00:16:47.480
neurodivergent because when we are very

00:16:45.200 --> 00:16:50.440
interested in something, the focus is

00:16:47.480 --> 00:16:52.240
like extreme. Nothing can stop us. One

00:16:50.440 --> 00:16:53.760
thing I do a lot is batching my work. I

00:16:52.240 --> 00:16:55.520
can come up with a few video ideas and I

00:16:53.760 --> 00:16:58.000
can prepare all of my research in a

00:16:55.520 --> 00:17:00.160
couple of days or I can make 50

00:16:58.000 --> 00:17:02.080
Instagram carousel posts in a few hours

00:17:00.160 --> 00:17:04.120
or edit 10 short videos for my Story

00:17:02.080 --> 00:17:06.920
Angels membership platform all at a

00:17:04.120 --> 00:17:08.520
time. Then, if I become allergic to it

00:17:06.920 --> 00:17:11.240
the next day, you know what? That's

00:17:08.520 --> 00:17:14.199
fine. As long as I have one or two days

00:17:11.240 --> 00:17:15.959
like this for each project once a month

00:17:14.199 --> 00:17:19.040
or at least whatever time period works

00:17:15.959 --> 00:17:21.600
for you, I'm set. You also need to do

00:17:19.040 --> 00:17:23.839
less and rest often in order to do more.

00:17:21.600 --> 00:17:26.520
Most people do not tell you this. I know

00:17:23.839 --> 00:17:28.319
a lot of us are all or nothing. And if

00:17:26.520 --> 00:17:30.080
that's you, stick to that because it

00:17:28.319 --> 00:17:32.520
works for us. Forget about the balance,

00:17:30.080 --> 00:17:34.400
okay? We can't all be hyper-focusing all

00:17:32.520 --> 00:17:36.800
day, every day. So, to avoid burning

00:17:34.400 --> 00:17:38.800
out, you can either have a hundred day

00:17:36.800 --> 00:17:40.720
or a zero day and do not even stress

00:17:38.800 --> 00:17:42.640
about it. Okay, the balance thing, if it

00:17:40.720 --> 00:17:44.200
doesn't work for you, again, that's for

00:17:42.640 --> 00:17:46.360
neurotypicals, which is what we're not.

00:17:44.200 --> 00:17:48.640
So, you can also design your environment

00:17:46.360 --> 00:17:50.000
so that hyperfocus happens on the right

00:17:48.640 --> 00:17:51.840
things and not scrolling Facebook

00:17:50.000 --> 00:17:53.320
Marketplace. I'm talking to myself. You

00:17:51.840 --> 00:17:54.800
need to create your strategy based on

00:17:53.320 --> 00:17:57.000
what you know about your environment.

00:17:54.800 --> 00:17:59.360
You can trigger focus state by

00:17:57.000 --> 00:18:02.520
correlating work with a sensory signal

00:17:59.360 --> 00:18:04.280
like a specific perfume or make some tea

00:18:02.520 --> 00:18:05.760
and, you know, drink your favorite

00:18:04.280 --> 00:18:07.880
beverage and sparkling water when you're

00:18:05.760 --> 00:18:09.560
ready to work. It's literally like snap

00:18:07.880 --> 00:18:11.280
hypnosis. You can also create this thing

00:18:09.560 --> 00:18:13.280
called memory loops. For example, if I

00:18:11.280 --> 00:18:15.080
tell myself I edit videos very well in

00:18:13.280 --> 00:18:16.440
the afternoon and my desk is clean and

00:18:15.080 --> 00:18:18.800
it's totally quiet and I light an

00:18:16.440 --> 00:18:21.000
incense, boom, I'm so ready to edit

00:18:18.800 --> 00:18:22.640
videos. If I did it once and it worked

00:18:21.000 --> 00:18:24.960
well, I can do it again and again and

00:18:22.640 --> 00:18:26.680
create that memory loop. Eventually, I'm

00:18:24.960 --> 00:18:28.360
going to automatically think, well, it's

00:18:26.680 --> 00:18:30.680
afternoon, I'm in my desk, I'm going to

00:18:28.360 --> 00:18:32.560
be so productive. Another thing that

00:18:30.680 --> 00:18:34.440
happens to us or at least to me is I

00:18:32.560 --> 00:18:36.280
used to misplace things a lot and then

00:18:34.440 --> 00:18:37.800
realize I'm holding it the whole time.

00:18:36.280 --> 00:18:40.000
It will help you to know where things

00:18:37.800 --> 00:18:42.000
should go. Have a dedicated space for

00:18:40.000 --> 00:18:45.040
different categories of items so you

00:18:42.000 --> 00:18:47.000
always know hair products go there. My

00:18:45.040 --> 00:18:48.680
keys are always either by the door or in

00:18:47.000 --> 00:18:50.480
my bag. There's no other place for it.

00:18:48.680 --> 00:18:52.240
There's only two places where it can go.

00:18:50.480 --> 00:18:54.120
And then make sure you always return it

00:18:52.240 --> 00:18:55.360
to that place. And let the people around

00:18:54.120 --> 00:18:56.880
you know that as well because if

00:18:55.360 --> 00:18:58.800
somebody else comes in and distracts

00:18:56.880 --> 00:19:00.720
myself, I will go insane. So, remind

00:18:58.800 --> 00:19:02.280
them, "Hey, this is where all the tools

00:19:00.720 --> 00:19:03.640
are supposed to go. Here's the drawer

00:19:02.280 --> 00:19:04.480
where all the scissors are." And then

00:19:03.640 --> 00:19:06.560
there's another thing we're all

00:19:04.480 --> 00:19:08.200
sensitive to, which is noise. I hate

00:19:06.560 --> 00:19:09.920
when things are slightly too loud. Like

00:19:08.200 --> 00:19:11.640
sometimes I don't care for hours and

00:19:09.920 --> 00:19:13.880
then suddenly it needs to stop right

00:19:11.640 --> 00:19:15.720
now. So, if you're working or studying

00:19:13.880 --> 00:19:17.400
in a noisy environment and you can't

00:19:15.720 --> 00:19:19.280
focus, then you have to block out the

00:19:17.400 --> 00:19:21.600
noise with noise-canceling headphones.

00:19:19.280 --> 00:19:23.080
Go to a different room. Find a library.

00:19:21.600 --> 00:19:25.440
If you are at a co-working space or an

00:19:23.080 --> 00:19:27.360
office, get in the phone booth. I do

00:19:25.440 --> 00:19:29.560
this, too, if I'm trying to sleep or

00:19:27.360 --> 00:19:31.000
rest. I always have earplugs on me.

00:19:29.560 --> 00:19:32.960
Like, if I don't and I'm trying to sleep

00:19:31.000 --> 00:19:35.000
on the plane or somewhere loud, I will

00:19:32.960 --> 00:19:37.000
go crazy. But, the point is you're

00:19:35.000 --> 00:19:39.640
removing as much friction as possible so

00:19:37.000 --> 00:19:42.000
that you can get into a flow state or do

00:19:39.640 --> 00:19:43.560
the thing supposed to do. Focus music is

00:19:42.000 --> 00:19:45.680
a great trigger, as well. I like

00:19:43.560 --> 00:19:47.280
listening to 90s ambient music while I

00:19:45.680 --> 00:19:49.440
work, and then sometimes I listen to

00:19:47.280 --> 00:19:50.720
affirmations. I also like body doubling,

00:19:49.440 --> 00:19:52.080
meaning you're working with someone

00:19:50.720 --> 00:19:53.880
who's staying present with you. I have a

00:19:52.080 --> 00:19:56.320
body doubling event on my Discord every

00:19:53.880 --> 00:19:57.440
week, where we play soft music and stay

00:19:56.320 --> 00:20:00.240
online with each other and be

00:19:57.440 --> 00:20:02.280
productive. Another option, if music is

00:20:00.240 --> 00:20:04.480
distracting, is brown noise. I don't

00:20:02.280 --> 00:20:07.080
know why specifically brown noise works

00:20:04.480 --> 00:20:09.840
so well, but as soon as it comes on, I'm

00:20:07.080 --> 00:20:11.400
completely locked in. A friend of mine

00:20:09.840 --> 00:20:12.720
even tested this out for me one time,

00:20:11.400 --> 00:20:14.920
once I was so like stressed and

00:20:12.720 --> 00:20:16.720
overstimulated and I couldn't focus, and

00:20:14.920 --> 00:20:17.680
then suddenly I could, and I realized,

00:20:16.720 --> 00:20:19.160
"Oh,

00:20:17.680 --> 00:20:21.200
the brown noise is on." So, when it

00:20:19.160 --> 00:20:23.680
comes to productivity tools, my best one

00:20:21.200 --> 00:20:25.840
is honestly just notes app with folders.

00:20:23.680 --> 00:20:27.320
If it's for groceries, grocery folder.

00:20:25.840 --> 00:20:29.120
If it's an idea that I have for YouTube

00:20:27.320 --> 00:20:31.440
or my newsletter, it goes into the

00:20:29.120 --> 00:20:33.560
YouTube and newsletter folder. The other

00:20:31.440 --> 00:20:35.400
great tools that I use are Notion and X

00:20:33.560 --> 00:20:37.360
1000 and the template, super super

00:20:35.400 --> 00:20:38.800
minimal templates. I know a lot of you

00:20:37.360 --> 00:20:40.960
guys are overwhelmed just by thinking

00:20:38.800 --> 00:20:43.040
about productivity systems, but do what

00:20:40.960 --> 00:20:44.280
works for you. I made a Notion tutorial

00:20:43.040 --> 00:20:45.840
video, as well, if you're interested,

00:20:44.280 --> 00:20:48.160
and that one is specifically made for

00:20:45.840 --> 00:20:49.960
people who do feel overwhelmed. So, the

00:20:48.160 --> 00:20:52.720
idea is we need to work with a clean

00:20:49.960 --> 00:20:55.080
slate, as simple as possible. Then, from

00:20:52.720 --> 00:20:56.760
there, we can improvise however we want.

00:20:55.080 --> 00:20:58.680
I also like to add little icons and

00:20:56.760 --> 00:21:00.480
emojis, so I can tell the difference

00:20:58.680 --> 00:21:01.920
between my projects. Cuz if not, for

00:21:00.480 --> 00:21:03.200
some reason, I can't even tell the

00:21:01.920 --> 00:21:05.160
difference. I don't know why. But, you

00:21:03.200 --> 00:21:06.720
will come up with your own ways to lock

00:21:05.160 --> 00:21:08.320
in, but I know that a lot of these tips

00:21:06.720 --> 00:21:10.720
in this video will help you build that.

00:21:08.320 --> 00:21:13.200
You can unlock so much when you know

00:21:10.720 --> 00:21:14.840
yourself and you're okay with yourself.

00:21:13.200 --> 00:21:17.680
And when you're okay with yourself, your

00:21:14.840 --> 00:21:20.080
weird little idiosyncrasies, then you

00:21:17.680 --> 00:21:22.200
will only be doing things your own way,

00:21:20.080 --> 00:21:24.000
which works for you. Lastly, do not

00:21:22.200 --> 00:21:26.080
underestimate trying to at least

00:21:24.000 --> 00:21:27.960
intentionally choosing to stop

00:21:26.080 --> 00:21:29.360
guilt-tripping or shaming yourself or

00:21:27.960 --> 00:21:30.480
holding yourself to high standards.

00:21:29.360 --> 00:21:31.920
Because typically when we're doing that,

00:21:30.480 --> 00:21:33.280
we're not doing ourselves a favor. If

00:21:31.920 --> 00:21:35.520
you do that, you're only making it

00:21:33.280 --> 00:21:38.200
worse. So, do the complete opposite and

00:21:35.520 --> 00:21:40.320
be super kind to yourself. The main goal

00:21:38.200 --> 00:21:41.640
of our lives, honestly, is to have fun,

00:21:40.320 --> 00:21:44.040
okay? So, just figure out whatever you

00:21:41.640 --> 00:21:45.920
can let go of and surrender and create

00:21:44.040 --> 00:21:47.960
some structure or boundaries around rest

00:21:45.920 --> 00:21:50.000
and fun and find something that works

00:21:47.960 --> 00:21:53.400
for you and get to know yourself. You're

00:21:50.000 --> 00:21:55.280
not a work robot. You're a human, so try

00:21:53.400 --> 00:21:56.760
and treat yourself like one. I hope all

00:21:55.280 --> 00:21:58.280
of these tips are helpful for you today.

00:21:56.760 --> 00:22:00.400
If you need a reminder, you can always

00:21:58.280 --> 00:22:02.520
download that PDF, which is a companion

00:22:00.400 --> 00:22:04.160
guide for this video specifically. It's

00:22:02.520 --> 00:22:05.800
free in the link in the description.

